WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.840
Nowadays, index fund investing is one of the
most popular and reliable ways to grow your

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.670
wealth over the long term.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.019
And given the high diversification, the low
volatility, and the extreme ease of index

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:13.599
funds, it’s no wonder they’re so popular.

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:17.600
But index funds aren’t a classic investment
tool like you might think.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.749
Just 30 years ago, index funds were largely
avoided, and 50 years ago, index fund investing

00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:23.749
wasn’t even a thing.

00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:28.869
It wasn’t until 1972 that the world’s
first index fund was created.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:33.560
Vanguard entered the scene a few years later
in 1975, and since then they’ve grown to

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.750
be the king of index funds.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:42.220
The largest index fund today is actually the
SPDR SPY ETF, but this is more of an anomaly.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:47.050
If you look right below Spy, you’ll see
that 5 out of the top 10 funds are from Vanguard

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.590
and that 31 out of the top 100 funds are also
from Vanguard.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.510
So, here’s how Vanguard became the second
largest investment firm in the world by just

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.790
embracing average.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:02.820
It all started with a man named John C Bogle
who was born on May 8, 1929 right before the

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.180
great depression started.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.069
John’s family, unfortunately, got annihilated
by the great depression.

00:01:08.069 --> 00:01:11.289
His parents lost all of their savings and
they were forced to sell their house.

00:01:11.289 --> 00:01:16.329
John’s father basically gave up on life
and became an alcoholic which resulted in

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:17.460
John’s parents divorcing.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:23.539
So, from a very young age, John was on his
own and his clearest path to success was education.

00:01:23.539 --> 00:01:27.789
John and his twin brother David both worked
extremely hard at school to try to get ahead,

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:29.780
and this would end up paying off.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.719
John’s stellar academic record earned him
a spot at an elite boarding school named Blair

00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:36.209
Academy on a full-ride work scholarship.

00:01:36.209 --> 00:01:43.159
Today, Blair Academy charges $66,500 per year
and they only have a total of 462 students.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:48.030
So, I think it’s safe to assume that they
don’t give these spots very easily.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.139
Anyway, John continued to excel at Blair and
he would eventually get accepted into Princeton.

00:01:53.139 --> 00:01:57.200
At Princeton, John majored in economics and
investments and it didn’t take him long

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.789
to delve into the world of mutual funds.

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:03.770
During his junior and senior years in college,
John wrote a graduating thesis paper called

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.599
“The Economic Role of the Investment Company”.

00:02:06.599 --> 00:02:09.720
This paper would actually get John his first
job.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.330
The story goes that Walter L Morgan, the founder
of the Wellington fund read John’s 130-page

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.740
paper and that he hired John right after.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:22.550
John quickly became a star at the fund and
by 1955, John was promoted to being an assistant

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.660
manager of the fund.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.690
John also pushed for the creation of a new
fund that was less concentrated than Wellington’s

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:29.690
other funds.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:33.240
Eventually, John would get his way and this
new fund turned out to be a great success.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.090
Soon enough, Walter Morgan would retire from
the company in 1970 and he would choose John

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.690
to be his successor.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.010
Things were going phenomenally for John.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.850
He had become the leader of a well-respected
fund at a rather young age of 41 years old.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.980
But, unfortunately, John would make a fateful
decision that would get him ousted from the

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.230
company.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:57.080
In the 1970s, John approved a merger that
turned out to be a disaster, and John would

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.930
be fired soon after.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:03.050
Though this was likely a terrible experience,
what’s surprising is that John never complained

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.460
about it or tried to make excuses.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:10.160
In fact, he describes the mistake as quote
“shameful and inexcusable and a reflection

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.120
of immaturity and confidence beyond what the
facts justified.”

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.310
It’s crazy how down to earth and humble
John was despite all of his success.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:22.700
John decided to start his own investment firm
named Vanguard in 1974 and that brings us

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.300
to the creation of the index fund.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:29.210
John is often credited with inventing the
world’s first index fund, but this isn’t

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.160
actually the case.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.290
The first idea for an index funds dates back
to two University of Chicago students.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.750
Edward Renshaw and Paul Feldstein suggested
the idea of creating an unmanaged investment

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:45.570
company way back in 1960, but the idea barely
got any support and it didn’t go anywhere.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:49.970
A decade later though, a company named Qualidex
Fund Inc would make their vision a reality

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:54.780
by creating a fund that tracked the Dow Jones
Industrial Average on July 31, 1972.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.610
So, John was not the first to create an index
fund, but he’s still no doubt the father

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.630
of index fund investing given that he was
the one to popularize it.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:06.970
John launched Vanguard’s first index fund
called the First Index Investment Trust on

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.610
December 31, 1975.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:14.060
The success of Vanguard didn’t just happen
overnight though because there was a massive

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:15.060
problem.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.750
In the 1970s, there was very little respect
for index fund investing within wall street.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:24.700
Fund managers thought that the idea of index
funds was whimsical and utterly pointless.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.620
It wasn’t just fund managers saying this
either, most investors held a similar belief

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.740
due to 2 key reasons.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.110
First of all, investors felt that they could
just do it themselves if they wanted to.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.630
All an index fund is doing is splitting the
money you invest across dozens or hundreds

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:41.730
of companies depending on what the fund is
tracking.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.610
Investors could easily just do that themselves.

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.860
They could call up their brokerage and tell
them to split their money across Nasdaq stocks

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:51.010
or S&amp;P 500 stocks.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:55.240
The investor wouldn’t even have to go through
the tediousness of buying 500 different stocks,

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.220
that was the brokerages’ problem.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.410
The second argument against index funds was
that it was quote and quote unamerican and

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.930
counterintuitive.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:07.740
People invest with fund managers to beat the
market, not to match the market.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.650
This is why the chairman of Fidelity Edward
Johnson said that he couldn’t believe that

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.690
the great mass of investors would be satisfied
with receiving just average returns.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:20.180
And these fund managers were right about Vanguard
at least at the start.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:24.560
The launch of the First Index Investment Trust
was a massive disappointment.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:29.860
John was hoping to raise $150 million during
the IPO but they only ended up raising a measly

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.440
$11 million.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.680
Now, you might be thinking $11 million isn’t
that bad.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.490
But the thing to keep in mind is that Vanguard
is just a manager of assets which means that

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:43.760
they’re only paid a very small percentage
of what they manage.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:49.010
Even if they charged 1% which is super high
for a passive fund, they would only make $110,000

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:50.130
per year.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:54.200
And that money isn’t just for John, that
money needs to support the operations of the

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.630
entire company.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:06:00.190
Fortunately, Vanguard was just a group of
three people including John and two analysts,

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:02.680
so the overhead costs were that large.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:06.160
Nonetheless, the IPO was extremely disappointing.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.930
The banks that helped John complete the IPO
advised that he should shut down the fund

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.210
given the poor reception, but John refused.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:16.730
John strongly believed that index fund investing
would become more popular for two of his own

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:17.810
reasons.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.371
His first argument was that the fees of an
index fund just had to be low enough for it

00:06:21.371 --> 00:06:23.320
to be worth investors’ time.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.310
The S&amp;P 500 adds companies and kicks out companies
on a quarterly basis.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:31.419
So, if you’re trying to track the S&amp;P 500
on your own, you would have to rebalance your

00:06:31.419 --> 00:06:33.169
portfolio every quarter.

00:06:33.169 --> 00:06:37.570
Also, every time you wanted to add money in
or take money out of your portfolio, you would

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:41.170
have to buy or sell the stocks of 500 different
companies.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:45.450
And whether it’s the investor doing it or
the broker doing it on behalf of the investor,

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:49.669
someone has to spend the time and money to
accurately track the desired index.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:53.710
If you were able to offer this service for
a silly low price, investors and brokers may

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:55.550
very well be interested.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.889
Since the start of Vanguard, one of the key
characteristics has been their stupid low

00:06:58.889 --> 00:06:59.889
fees.

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:06.400
The annual fee on their S&amp;P 500 fund VOO for
instance is only 0.03% per year.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:11.000
This means that for every $10,000 you invest,
Vanguard only charges $3 per year.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.930
I think you can see why this is quite attractive.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:18.169
John’s second belief was that over the long
term, actively managed funds would lose to

00:07:18.169 --> 00:07:19.169
the market.

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:23.540
And over time, we’ve seen that this is indeed
the case with most funds.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:28.840
But in the late 1970s, John’s low fees and
long-term outlook weren’t very convincing.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:33.330
At the end of the first year, the index fund
had only grown to $17 million.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:38.229
But eventually, when the bull market started
in 1982 after the inflation crisis of the

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:42.210
1970s, the indices finally started to grow
at a substantial rate.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:46.160
This significantly boosted the interest in
index fund investing, but index funds were

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.040
still largely avoided.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:52.950
Something that did help Vanguard though was
the start of the 401k era in the 1908s.

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.690
Instead of directly targeting investors and
brokerages with their index funds, Vanguard

00:07:56.690 --> 00:07:58.850
switched its focus to companies.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:02.080
Unlike investors and wall street who were
concerned about beating the market and generating

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.080
the greatest returns possible, companies were
more concerned about giving their employees

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.400
a low-cost, reliable, and stable investment
option, and index funds were perfect for this.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.699
Vanguard became a top choice for employee
retirement funds and 401k plans which allowed

00:08:15.699 --> 00:08:18.100
Vanguard to finally gain some traction.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.979
Vanguard didn’t stop targeting direct investors
either though.

00:08:20.979 --> 00:08:23.990
They actually came out with more options for
direct investors.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:29.230
In December of 1986, Vanguard launched their
second fund which was the Total Bond Fund.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:33.610
In December of 1987, Vanguard launched the
extended market index fund which tracks the

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:34.850
entire stock market.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:39.839
Since then, Vanguard has continuously launched
fund after fund each with a different focus

00:08:39.839 --> 00:08:42.800
such as small market cap companies and commodities.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:46.850
All of these new funds combined with the rise
of retirement funds, Vanguard blew up during

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:52.660
the 1980s and they ended the decade with $55.8
billion in assets under management.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:56.940
For most funds, this would be a fantastic
amount but given Vanguard’s extremely low

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:01.350
fee structure, they needed much more to make
the same amount of money as a regular $55.8

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:02.820
billion funds.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:08.140
With a 0.03% fee, for instance, Vanguard would
only be pulling in $16.7 million per year

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.570
despite managing $55.8 billion.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:14.560
Fortunately, though, Vanguard’s assets would
continue to grow exponentially throughout

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:16.070
the 1990s.

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:20.120
The key development in the 1990s and early
2000s was the rise of tech companies and the

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.600
dot-com bubble.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.200
Tech companies paid their employees extremely
well which made managing their retirements

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.670
funds extremely lucrative.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:31.620
By 2005, Vanguard’s assets under management
grew to just under $1 trillion.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:36.720
In the meantime, John retired from Vanguard
in 1999 as he reached the mandatory retirement

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.080
age at the company of 70.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:42.640
Since John’s retirement, the new leaders
of Vanguard have placed more focus on actively

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.180
managed funds, but ironically, this is when
the popularity of index funds went through

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:48.300
the roof.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:52.880
In the late 2000s, Warren Buffett made his
famous bet against hedge funds, and retail

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.290
investors have been more than happy to take
it easy and simply enjoy market returns.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:01.040
This newfound retail interest has skyrocketed
Vanguard’s total assets under management

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:04.480
to a whopping $7.5 trillion.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:09.290
But what I think is even more impressive than
their crazy balance sheet is their ethics.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:13.589
Wall Street often gets a bad rep for terrible
ethics and most of the criticism they get

00:10:13.589 --> 00:10:14.800
is well deserved.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.880
But the same cannot be said about Vanguard.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:21.440
The only profit Vanguard makes is the measly
fees they charge on their funds.

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.670
Now, of course, they manage trillions, so
they make plenty of money.

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:30.350
But what I’m referring to is the fact that
Vanguard doesn’t actually have any investors.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:34.140
John strongly believed that it was a major
conflict of interest to offer stock in a company

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:38.200
that was meant to generate the best returns
possible for their fund investors.

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:41.330
Every dollar the company makes is one less
dollar that could’ve been paid out to the

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:42.779
fund investors.

00:10:42.779 --> 00:10:47.250
So, John decided that Vanguard would have
no ownership whatsoever.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:50.950
Vanguard doesn’t have any stock and it’s
not privately owned.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:55.990
Instead, Vanguard likes to say that the company
is owned by all the investors of their funds.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:59.529
To put in perspective how much Vanguard is
giving up with this decision, let’s take

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:01.070
a look at Blackrock.

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:05.450
Blackrock is the largest asset manager in
the world and they manage over $9 trillion

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:06.649
worth of assets.

00:11:06.649 --> 00:11:11.690
Blackrock does have stock and its market cap
is $138 billion.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:15.640
Even if we said that Vanguard was only valued
half as much as Blackrock, that would be about

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:17.500
$70 billion.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:21.899
John could’ve easily held onto 25% of the
company which would’ve given him a net worth

00:11:21.899 --> 00:11:24.920
of $17.5 billion.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:28.450
But instead, John died with a net worth of
only $80 million.

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:33.100
Obviously, that’s not a bad amount, but
for someone who literally created trillions

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:37.470
of dollars of value for American investors,
that’s an awfully modest salary.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:41.410
Something else I wanted to note is that John
gave half of his salary to charity.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:46.620
Truly quite an inspiring story, but we’ll
have to see how long Vanguard upholds John’s

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:49.990
ethics because John, unfortunately, passed
away 2 years ago in 2019.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:54.460
I’m sure his legacy with Vanguard, however,
will live on forever.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:56.640
What do you guys think about Vanguard’s
business model?

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:57.970
Comment that down below.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:01.370
Also, drop a like if John’s ethics inspire
you.

